 ## Overview
-SubSplat is a method for high-resolution novel-view synthesis that resolves the trade-off between network computational cost and rendering fidelity in pixel-aligned 3D Gaussian Splatting. It solves the problem of insufficient Gaussian density and artifacts when using low-resolution backbone inputs by introducing a Sub-pixel Gaussian Reparameterizer (SPGR). Unlike prior methods limited by fixed grids or image-space upsamplers, SubSplat subdivides each grid-anchored primary Gaussian into multiple fine-grained sub-pixel primitives, restoring structural density directly from low-resolution features while maintaining stable network latency [S1].
+SubSplat is a method for high-resolution novel-view synthesis that resolves the trade-off between network computational cost and rendering fidelity in pixel-aligned 3D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. It solves the problem of insufficient Gaussian density and artifacts when using low-resolution backbone inputs by introducing a Sub-pixel Gaussian Reparameterizer (SPGR) [S1]. Unlike prior methods limited by fixed grids or image-space upsamplers, SubSplat subdivides each grid-anchored primary Gaussian into multiple fine-grained sub-pixel primitives, restoring structural density directly from low-resolution features while maintaining stable network latency [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-SubSplat builds on the [AnySplat Pipeline](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) by refining the Gaussian generation stage. It takes low-resolution multi-view features and primary Gaussian anchors as input, and outputs a set of sub-pixel Gaussians suitable for high-resolution rendering.
+SubSplat builds on the [AnySplat Pipeline](12847-anysplat-pipeline.md) by refining the Gaussian generation stage. It takes low-resolution multi-view features and primary Gaussian anchors as input, and outputs a set of sub-pixel Gaussians suitable for high-resolution rendering [S1].
 
